WEBVTT

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:07.540
<v Sarah Okafor>Morning, everyone. Monday the fifth, first one of 2026. Can you all hear me?</v>

00:00:10.029 --> 00:00:13.880
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, just about. Had Teams coming through the wrong speaker again.</v>

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:21.760
<v Sarah Okafor>Lovely. Did anyone's weekend plans survive that weather? Mine mostly became sitting indoors watching it rain.</v>

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.829
<v David Whitfield>Went for a walk. Fairly quickly became a walk back to the car.</v>

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:35.079
<v Sarah Okafor>Sounds familiar. My plans for next weekend now depend entirely on whether my coat dries.</v>

00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:43.869
<v Priya Raman>On a brighter note, there's a birthday cake in the office. Already there, before anyone thinks I'm offering to bake.</v>

00:00:45.859 --> 00:00:48.659
<v Hannah Price>Is that the one in the massive tin?</v>

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:52.299
<v Priya Raman>Yeah. Tin's mostly cake, not mostly packaging for once.</v>

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.649
<v Grace Liu>A rare bit of good news for a Monday.</v>

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:02.909
<v Sarah Okafor>Right, before we turn this into a cake meeting. Finished bits first?</v>

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:08.409
<v Hannah Price>Yeah so, tested the cooling tower yesterday and it's all fine now.</v>

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.349
<v Sarah Okafor>That's finished, then, not something rolling into this morning?</v>

00:01:13.549 --> 00:01:15.650
<v Hannah Price>Finished. Nothing outstanding from that test.</v>

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:22.400
<v Hannah Price>And I tested the GT1 Compressor Section yesterday and it's all fine now as well. Both done.</v>

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:27.079
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, understood. Nice to have those off the list.</v>

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:33.730
<v Sarah Okafor>One observation from nights: noticed the water booster was running a bit warm, but it settled down.</v>

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.840
<v David Whitfield>Settled before the end of nights, you mean?</v>

00:01:38.189 --> 00:01:43.090
<v Sarah Okafor>Yeah. Just passing on what was seen, not raising another job out of it.</v>

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:51.840
<v Marcus Hale>While we're on status, I still can't get to the air dryer until production gives us a window.</v>

00:01:52.189 --> 00:01:56.390
<v Sarah Okafor>Right, that's still blocked. Not calling that an agreed job for today.</v>

00:01:56.799 --> 00:01:59.950
<v Marcus Hale>Exactly. No window, no access. That's where it sits.</v>

00:02:01.569 --> 00:02:07.519
<v David Whitfield>And the supplier still hasn't confirmed the delivery for the line one HPU. Same blocker as before.</v>

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:12.090
<v Aisha Begum>Yeah, that delivery's still unconfirmed, then.</v>

00:02:13.389 --> 00:02:18.289
<v Sarah Okafor>Okay. Blocker noted, no fresh action on that in this meeting. Actual jobs now.</v>

00:02:18.949 --> 00:02:25.599
<v Sarah Okafor>Marcus Hale, can you inspect the GT1 fuel valve for leaks by the 28th? Whenever you get a chance.</v>

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:33.810
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, I'll inspect the GT1 fuel valve for leaks by the 28th. Fit it in whenever I get a chance.</v>

00:02:34.539 --> 00:02:39.789
<v Grace Liu>Just to be clear, that's a routine inspection request, not someone reporting an active leak?</v>

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.879
<v Sarah Okafor>Correct, no active leak being reported here. It's the inspection we're agreeing.</v>

00:02:46.189 --> 00:02:49.340
<v Marcus Hale>Understood. That's mine, with the 28th as the deadline.</v>

00:02:51.289 --> 00:02:55.840
<v David Whitfield>Right. Plenty on these lists ends up sounding like it's all top priority.</v>

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:01.479
<v Sarah Okafor>Yeah, and it isn't. Same distinction on the conveyor.</v>

00:03:03.199 --> 00:03:09.849
<v Sarah Okafor>PR, can you grease and check the bearings on the Main Outfeed Conveyor? It matters but it's not urgent.</v>

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.659
<v Priya Raman>Grease and check the bearings, yeah? Not just a grease round.</v>

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:21.259
<v Sarah Okafor>That's it, both. It matters but it's not urgent.</v>

00:03:21.879 --> 00:03:27.129
<v Priya Raman>Okay, I'll grease and check the bearings on the Main Outfeed Conveyor. I've got that.</v>

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:34.300
<v David Whitfield>Yeah, greasing something and knowing the bearing's all right aren't quite the same thing.</v>

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:37.569
<v Priya Raman>Mm, exactly. Understood.</v>

00:03:38.189 --> 00:03:45.189
<v Sarah Okafor>Han, can you run a function test on the line 2 interlock? Not an emergency but don't let it slip.</v>

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:50.159
<v Hannah Price>Yeah. Separate from the two tests I mentioned earlier, obviously.</v>

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:59.370
<v Sarah Okafor>Yes, those are closed. This is a separate function test on the line 2 now being put on the list.</v>

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:07.860
<v Hannah Price>Fine, I'll run a function test on the line 2 interlock. Not an emergency, but I won't let it slip.</v>

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:13.199
<v Grace Liu>That's a useful distinction. Not an emergency doesn't mean forgotten about.</v>

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:17.209
<v Hannah Price>No, agreed. It's on mine.</v>

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:23.759
<v Sarah Okafor>Aisha, can you chase the purchase order for the CV01 today at 2pm? Fairly important, that one.</v>

00:04:25.839 --> 00:04:29.689
<v Aisha Begum>Sorry, you clipped a bit. Today at 2pm for the chase?</v>

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:33.009
<v Sarah Okafor>Yes, today at 2pm. It's fairly important.</v>

00:04:33.389 --> 00:04:37.939
<v Aisha Begum>Got you. I'll chase the purchase order for the CV01 today at 2pm.</v>

00:04:38.399 --> 00:04:42.250
<v David Whitfield>And that's separate from the delivery confirmation I was on about?</v>

00:04:43.389 --> 00:04:50.040
<v Aisha Begum>Yeah, separate. I'm taking the purchase order chase for the CV01, not another supplier chase for the blocked delivery.</v>

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:58.529
<v Aisha Begum>And I'll sort the permit to work and isolation for the line one at half two. That's fairly important as well.</v>

00:04:59.819 --> 00:05:04.720
<v Grace Liu>Sorry, talking over you there—you're taking both the permit to work and the isolation?</v>

00:05:07.079 --> 00:05:13.029
<v Aisha Begum>Yeah, both. I'll sort the permit to work and isolation for the line one at half two.</v>

00:05:13.959 --> 00:05:17.459
<v Sarah Okafor>Thanks, Aisha. Agreed, that's yours. Fairly important, as you say.</v>

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:26.879
<v Aisha Begum>And just so the two conversations don't get mixed up, that's not me saying production's given us access to the air dryer.</v>

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.199
<v Marcus Hale>No, definitely not. That one's still blocked.</v>

00:05:32.459 --> 00:05:42.259
<v Sarah Okafor>Right. Aisha, just confirming those two times: purchase order chase for the CV01 today at 2pm; permit to work and isolation for the line one at half two.</v>

00:05:43.709 --> 00:05:46.860
<v Aisha Begum>Yep, agreed. Both with me, and both fairly important.</v>

00:05:48.689 --> 00:05:53.240
<v Priya Raman>Good. The cake, for the avoidance of doubt, has no purchase order attached.</v>

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:55.769
<v David Whitfield>That'll be why it actually arrived.</v>

00:05:57.850 --> 00:06:03.100
<v Sarah Okafor>Ha. Right, we've got the agreements and the blocked bits are still blocked. Thanks, everyone.</v>
